Relevant columns and their usage for the requested analysis  
• `scientificname`: identify the exact species (“Sterna paradisaea”).  
• `species`: alternative, usually identical to the binomial part of `scientificname`; can also be used in the filter.  
• `decimallatitude`: geographic filter – keep only records north of 40 ° N (value > 40).  
• `year`, `month`, `day`: already parsed parts of the collection date – convenient for calendar filtering and grouping.  
• `eventdate`: full timestamp – can be cast to a DATE when needed.  
• `kingdom`, `phylum`, `class`, `order`, `family`, `genus`: taxonomy columns; `genus` is helpful for quick checks but final filter must be species specific.  
• `taxonrank`: make sure the record really refers to a species‐level determination (`taxonrank = 'SPECIES'`).  
• `individualcount`: (not strictly necessary) could help remove obviously wrong zero‑count records.  
Conditions that will be useful  
1. LOWER(`species`) = 'sterna paradisaea'   (or the same on `scientificname`).  
2. `decimallatitude` > 40.  
3. `month` > 1   (exclude January).  
4. Aggregate by `DATE(year,month,day)` and use HAVING COUNT(*) > 10.  

Below are ten exploratory‐to‑final queries.  
Every query is a plain SELECT (no DDL, no schema inspection) on the only allowed table:  
`bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`.  
Each one is limited to 20 rows and is wrapped in back‑ticks; a brief description precedes each.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at distinct species names that start with “Sterna”.
SELECT DISTINCT species
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) LIKE 'sterna%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Sample 20 Arctic Tern records to verify spelling and fields.
SELECT gbifid, species, scientificname, decimallatitude, year, month, day
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Same sample, but only north of 40 ° N.
SELECT gbifid, species, decimallatitude, year, month, day
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
  AND decimallatitude > 40
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Inspect the distribution of months for the species north of 40 ° N.
SELECT DISTINCT month
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
  AND decimallatitude > 40
  AND month IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Count daily sightings (all years) after January, north of 40 ° N.
SELECT DATE(year,month,day) AS obs_date,
       COUNT(*) AS records
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
  AND decimallatitude > 40
  AND month > 1
  AND year IS NOT NULL AND month IS NOT NULL AND day IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY obs_date
ORDER BY obs_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Same as #5 but keep only days with >10 sightings.
SELECT DATE(year,month,day) AS obs_date,
       COUNT(*) AS records
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
  AND decimallatitude > 40
  AND month > 1
  AND year IS NOT NULL AND month IS NOT NULL AND day IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY obs_date
HAVING COUNT(*) > 10
ORDER BY obs_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Find, for each year, the first date after January with >10 sightings (>40 ° N).
SELECT year,
       MIN(DATE(year,month,day)) AS first_big_sighting_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
  AND decimallatitude > 40
  AND month > 1
  AND year IS NOT NULL AND month IS NOT NULL AND day IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY year
HAVING COUNTIF(month > 1) > 0
QUALIFY
  -- keep only those dates whose daily count is >10
  TRUE
ORDER BY year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) More exact version of #7 using a sub‑query to apply the >10 filter.
WITH daily AS (
  SELECT year, month, day,
         DATE(year,month,day) AS obs_date,
         COUNT(*) AS records
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
  WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
    AND decimallatitude > 40
    AND month > 1
    AND year IS NOT NULL AND month IS NOT NULL AND day IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY year, month, day
  HAVING COUNT(*) > 10
)
SELECT year,
       MIN(obs_date) AS first_big_sighting_date
FROM daily
GROUP BY year
ORDER BY first_big_sighting_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Identify the single earliest first‑big‑sighting date across all years.
WITH daily AS (
  SELECT year, month, day,
         DATE(year,month,day) AS obs_date,
         COUNT(*) AS records
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
  WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
    AND decimallatitude > 40
    AND month > 1
    AND year IS NOT NULL AND month IS NOT NULL AND day IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY year, month, day
  HAVING COUNT(*) > 10
),
per_year AS (
  SELECT year,
         MIN(obs_date) AS first_big_sighting_date
  FROM daily
  GROUP BY year
)
SELECT *
FROM per_year
ORDER BY first_big_sighting_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) Final answer: which year had the earliest qualifying date?
WITH daily AS (
  SELECT year, month, day,
         DATE(year,month,day) AS obs_date,
         COUNT(*) AS records
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
  WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'
    AND decimallatitude > 40
    AND month > 1
    AND year IS NOT NULL AND month IS NOT NULL AND day IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY year, month, day
  HAVING COUNT(*) > 10
),
per_year AS (
  SELECT year,
         MIN(obs_date) AS first_big_sighting_date
  FROM daily
  GROUP BY year
)
SELECT year,
       first_big_sighting_date
FROM per_year
ORDER BY first_big_sighting_date
LIMIT 20;  -- will return the earliest date; the first row’s year is the answer
```

The first row returned by Query 10 gives the requested year as well as that earliest >10‑sighting date.
