The problem can be solved in three logical steps:

1. Identify daily “big‑sighting” dates  
   • Filter to Arctic Tern (`LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'`)  
   • Restrict to records north of 40 °N (`decimallatitude > 40`)  
   • Exclude January (`month > 1`)  
   • Group by date (`year, month, day`) and keep only those with more than 10 records.

2. For each year, find the first (minimum) such date.

3. Order the yearly results by that date so the row with the earliest date appears first; its year is the answer.

The CTE structure makes those steps explicit.

```sql
--Description: Earliest post‑January day (north of 40 °N) with >10 Arctic‑Tern
--             sightings for each year; ordered so the very first row is the answer year.
WITH daily_counts AS (                         -- Step 1: daily aggregation
  SELECT
    year,
    DATE(year, month, day) AS obs_date,
    COUNT(*) AS records
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.gbif.occurrences`
  WHERE LOWER(species) = 'sterna paradisaea'    -- Arctic Tern
    AND decimallatitude > 40                   -- north of 40 °N
    AND month > 1                              -- after January
    AND year  IS NOT NULL                     -- ensure complete dates
    AND month IS NOT NULL
    AND day   IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY year, month, day
  HAVING COUNT(*) > 10                         -- “big‑sighting” threshold
),
per_year AS (                                  -- Step 2: first such day per year
  SELECT
    year,
    MIN(obs_date) AS first_big_sighting_date
  FROM daily_counts
  GROUP BY year
)
SELECT                                         -- Step 3: order to reveal answer
  year,
  first_big_sighting_date
FROM per_year
ORDER BY first_big_sighting_date               -- earliest first
LIMIT 20;                                      -- trims output; top row is the target year
```
